<p>International Energy Agency chief Fatih Birol urged governments not to hoard fuel or impose export restrictions as the war involving Iran and the closure of the Strait of Hormuz disrupt global oil flows. He stressed that strategic reserves exist to steady markets during a supply shock. Australia said key Asian partners expected normal fuel shipments to continue and that suppliers were still securing incoming cargoes. At the same time, transport and logistics groups said the disruption should push the country to rethink its long-term fuel resilience.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/83B5Dz0-t-doA1pUX-8j32Mvt4cHNftFI4we_gBdMJguqvlNjzWb0kfPHFAohZcN2xbgPOc2soRMQ2OW5bAt-8LQJ7w4C4Bb-7zgSv2J_dNkzxtJV8HIFMK-XuIQ3BEDeCvlJ76cmJaRZ7wQJR2kkRJmuIB2dkgRPWA' alt='Motorists face higher fuel prices as Australia works to shore up supply during global oil disruption.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Reserve levels: Australia&#x27;s reported fuel cover was 39 days for petrol, 29 days for diesel and 30 days for jet fuel amid continued strong demand.</li><li>Station outages: In Australia, the number of service stations without diesel fell to 312 out of about 8,000, suggesting some local supply pressure had eased despite the broader disruption.</li><li>Incoming cargoes: New orders more than replaced cancelled ones, with 3.7 billion litres booked for April and into May.</li><li>Market position: Logistics leaders said Australia is a small market at the end of major trade routes, leaving it exposed when ships are diverted to more profitable destinations.</li></ul><blockquote>This is the most serious global supply chain interruption to energy ever, and we need to see this in the context of Australia’s future - Hermione Parsons</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>International Energy Agency: Birol said emergency fuel stocks are meant to be used during a shock to keep markets functioning, not offset by new export restrictions. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/9e47e3b8-fae1-4c1c-b79c-22dda42bc2b1'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Australian government: Albanese&#x27;s government emphasized supplier outreach and incoming shipments as it worked to avert petrol and diesel shortages, rather than signaling panic measures. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/apr/05/asian-leaders-assure-australia-normal-supply-of-fuel-will-continue-as-albanese-focuses-on-averting-shortages'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Australian Logistics Council: The council said stronger fuel stocks should go hand in hand with a broader focus on supply-chain sustainability, resilience and productivity. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/how-australia-can-protect-against-future-fuel-shocks/'>Michael West Media</a>)</li><li>Transport and logistics experts: Industry voices said Australia should reduce its long-term dependence on imported liquid fuels by expanding options such as renewable diesel, electrification and freight rail. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/how-australia-can-protect-against-future-fuel-shocks/'>Michael West Media</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/9e47e3b8-fae1-4c1c-b79c-22dda42bc2b1'>Countries must not hoard fuel during Iran war, warns IEA</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/apr/05/asian-leaders-assure-australia-normal-supply-of-fuel-will-continue-as-albanese-focuses-on-averting-shortages'>Asian countries assure Australia ‘normal supply’ of fuel will continue as Albanese focuses on averting shortages</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/how-australia-can-protect-against-future-fuel-shocks/'>How Australia can protect against future fuel shocks</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li></ul>